WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Here we are comparing two different questions.

00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:08.237
The count function applied directly to a column counts values that are not null.

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:14.742
But a text value containing an empty string or only spaces is still technically not null.

00:00:14.742 --> 00:00:18.682
So we also create a nonblank version of the count.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:25.832
The query trims spaces from the text and uses the null if function to convert an empty result to null before counting

00:00:25.832 --> 00:00:26.423
it.

00:00:27.091 --> 00:00:31.462
For example, suppose a table has ten customer names.

00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:38.097
Eight rows contain visible names, one row contains null, and one row contains only spaces.

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:41.767
A simple non-null count would report nine.

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.497
A nonblank count would report eight.

00:00:43.497 --> 00:00:53.057
That difference is useful because it tells us that missingness can be represented in more than one way.

00:00:53.057 --> 00:01:00.297
When you run the query, compare the non-null and nonblank counts for the name, email, and phone columns.
